//! Skipping the text that holds no match.
//!
//! A place that gives no match costs the bytes that were read from it, and
//! the next place reads them again. The automaton of the ends reads every
//! byte once, for the matches from every place, and stops at the first
//! place where a match ends. The text before that place is passed over in
//! one reading, and a state of it that stays in itself has its run, so the
//! bytes are skipped sixteen at a time in every such state and not only
//! before a first byte:
//!
//! ```text
//! rule      [a-z]+ = [0-9]+
//! text      let total = base; rate=12
//! ends      ------------------------^      a match ends at 24: rate=1
//! idle                        ^            no match is begun at 18
//! match                       -------      the match, from 18 to 25
//! ```
//!
//! The automaton of the ends does not say which match ends there, and it
//! need not be the one that the search is for: `abcd|c` in `abcd` ends
//! first at 3, with the `c`, and the match is all four bytes. So the end
//! is only a bound. With `e` the first place where a match ends, the match
//! that begins first begins at a place `s` with
//!
//! ```text
//! max(idle, e - k) <= s <= e - m
//! ```
//!
//! where `m` and `k` are the least and the greatest length that the
//! shortest match from a place can have, as `Automaton::shortest_lengths`
//! gives them, and `idle` is the last place before `e` where the
//! automaton of the ends was in its start state: no match was begun
//! there, either because the search began there or because every match
//! begun before had failed.
//!
//! - `s <= e - m`: a match ends at `e`. It has `m` bytes or more, and it
//!   begins at `s` or after, the first place where a match begins.
//! - `e - k <= s`: the shortest match from `s` ends at `e` or after, since
//!   no match ends before `e`, and it has `k` bytes at most.
//! - `idle <= s`: a match that begins before `idle` ends after it, since
//!   no match ends before `e`. So at `idle` it is begun and has not
//!   failed, and the automaton of the ends is not in its start state.
//!
//! From the lower bound the searcher seeks the next first byte and matches
//! there, as it does without skipping, and what it finds is the match at
//! `s`: the first one from the bound on. When the lengths have no greatest
//! one, `idle` alone is the bound. When `m` is `k`, as for `[a-z]{8,13}`,
//! whose shortest match has eight bytes wherever it begins, `s` is `e - k`
//! and no place is tried in vain.
//!
//! The automaton of the ends is not asked at once. A place is tried as it
//! is, and where it has a match, as in a text that is one match after
//! another, nothing is in the way. A place that fails is not yet a reason
//! either: where the first bytes are few and far between, as for
//! `q[a-z]*[0-9]`, seeking the next one passes over the text sixteen bytes
//! at a time and a place costs a few bytes, while the automaton of the
//! ends steps through every word that a first byte begins. So a search
//! keeps an account, in bytes:
//!
//! ```text
//! a byte passed on the way to the next place     earns 1
//! a place that failed after reading n bytes      costs 4 + n / 2
//! ```
//!
//! While the credit pays for the places that fail, the next first byte is
//! sought and tried. At the first place that it does not pay for, the
//! automaton of the ends reads on from the byte after that place, up to
//! the first place where a match ends; the bound holds for the places
//! from there on, and the credit is spent. A stream begins with 64 to its
//! credit and keeps what is left from one match to the next, so a text
//! of words spends it once, and from then on the automaton of the ends is
//! asked at the first place that fails, unless the place was far. `find`
//! begins with none: it is one search.
//!
//! The 4 is what a place that fails was measured to cost, in bytes of
//! the automaton of the ends, where it reads a few bytes; a place that
//! reads far reads most of it sixteen bytes at a time.
//!
//! The bytes that places read in vain before the automaton of the ends is
//! asked are at most twice the credit: twice the bytes passed and the 64.
//! `a*b` in a text of `a` only is two readings, one of the first place
//! and one of the automaton of the ends. The bytes from the bound to the
//! end of the match are read once more, and more often where places from
//! the bound on fail, which rules can still make as many as the square
//! of the length: `a*bq|ba*c` in `aaa...baaa...c` is tried from every `a`
//! before the `b`.
//!
//! Once asked, the automaton of the ends reads up to the next end of a
//! match, however far that is and whatever the text is like on the way:
//! there it steps through the words that the first bytes begin, where
//! seeking would have passed over the text between them.
//!
//! A searcher is built to skip when a match can begin while one that
//! began before is still on its way, as `Automaton::overlaps` says, and
//! the shortest match from a place can have five bytes or more. Only
//! with the first are bytes read from several places: for
//! `[a-z]+ = [0-9]+` every letter of a word is tried and reads the rest
//! of the word. Without the second, as for `[a-z]{3}`, a place that
//! fails has two bytes of a match at most, and the automaton of the ends
//! would be asked for every such place to say what the next place says
//! as fast. The literal that every match begins with is sought as before,
//! whatever follows it.
//!
//! A searcher that seeks the sets of the first places, as `sets` has it,
//! keeps doing so under those two conditions, and keeps this account for
//! the places that it tries, with the credit of a stream for every
//! search: the automaton of the ends reads on from the first place that
//! the credit does not pay for, and the sets are sought again from the
//! bound.

import core::ptr::Generic;
import std::option::Option;
import std::regex::automaton::Automaton;
import std::regex::automaton::scan;
import std::regex::search::Searcher;
import std::regex::search::Way;

// What a stream has to its credit when it begins, in bytes: enough for
// the places of a few words, so that a text whose first bytes are far
// apart on the whole is not given up for the first few that are near.
pub fn allowance() -> u64 {
    64
}

impl Searcher {
    /// The searcher of the rules of `automaton` that skips with the
    /// automaton of the ends when that serves its rules, as `Way::Skip`
    /// says, or bounds with it the places that it seeks by their sets,
    /// as `Way::Sets` says. The search for the states of the automaton
    /// of the ends gives up when it has found more than `limit` of them,
    /// and the searcher then seeks as that of `new` does.
    pub fn skipping(automaton: Automaton, limit: u64) -> Searcher {
        let plain = Searcher::new(automaton);
        if plain.way.gains(automaton) {
            match automaton.ends(limit) {
                Ok(ends) => Searcher {
                    automaton,
                    // A searcher that seeks the sets goes on seeking
                    // them, and has the automaton of the ends for the
                    // places that its credit does not pay for.
                    way: match plain.way {
                        Sets => Way::Sets,
                        _ => Way::Skip
                    },
                    finder: plain.finder,
                    literal: plain.literal,
                    program: plain.program,
                    ends: Option::Some { ends },
                    reach: match automaton.shortest_lengths() {
                        Within(_, greatest) => greatest,
                        _ => 18446744073709551615
                    },
                    sets: plain.sets,
                    places: plain.places,
                    sought: plain.sought
                },
                Err(_) => plain
            }
        } else {
            plain
        }
    }

    // What a searcher that skips does before `sought`, for the first
    // match that begins at the byte `from` of the `length` bytes at
    // `bytes` or after it, with `credit` for the places that fail. The
    // next first byte is sought and tried for as long as the credit pays
    // for the places that have no match. With a match, or at the end of
    // the bytes, the answer is that of `sought` and the fourth is false.
    // At the first place that the credit does not pay for, the automaton
    // of the ends reads on from the byte after it. When a match ends in
    // the bytes, the fourth is true and the place is the bound for where
    // the match begins: `sought` from there finds it, since no place
    // before the bound has a match. When none ends, there is no match,
    // and the fourth is false. The last is the credit that is left.
    fn skipped(self: Self, bytes: ConstPtr<Generic>, length: u64, from: u64, credit: u64) -> (u32, u64, u64, bool, u64) {
        // The automaton is taken once, as in `sought`.
        let automaton = self.automaton;
        let (at, credit, last, start, end, _, paid) = while [
            at = from,
            credit = credit,
            last = 0,
            start = from,
            end = from,
            going = true,
            paid = true
        ] (going) {
            let place = self.place(bytes, length, at);
            let (last, end, read) = scan::tried(automaton, bytes, length, place);
            if last != 0 || place >= length {
                // The bytes up to the place are passed.
                (at, credit + (place - at), last, place, end, false, true)
            } else {
                let earned = credit + (place + 1 - at);
                let cost = 4 + (read - place) / 2;
                if earned >= cost {
                    (place + 1, earned - cost, last, place, end, true, true)
                } else {
                    (place + 1, 0, last, place, end, false, false)
                }
            }
        };
        if paid { (last, start, end, false, credit) } else {
            let (found, low) = self.bound(bytes, length, at);
            if found { (0, low, low, true, 0) } else { (0, from, from, false, 0) }
        }
    }

    // Whether a match begins at the byte `from` of the `length` bytes at
    // `bytes` or after it, and the earliest place where the first one can
    // begin: the automaton of the ends reads up to the first place where
    // a match ends, and the place is the bound that this place gives.
    // `sought` finds the match from there.
    fn bound(self: Self, bytes: ConstPtr<Generic>, length: u64, from: u64) -> (bool, u64) {
        let (found, end, idle) = self.ending(bytes, length, from);
        // The shortest match from the place where the match begins ends
        // at `end` or after.
        let far = if end > self.reach {
            end - self.reach
        } else {
            0
        };
        (found, if far > idle { far } else { idle })
    }

    // Whether a match ends in the `length` bytes at `bytes` that begins at
    // the byte `from` or after it, the first place where one does, and
    // the last place before it where none was begun.
    fn ending(self: Self, bytes: ConstPtr<Generic>, length: u64, from: u64) -> (bool, u64, u64) {
        match self.ends {
            Some(ends) => scan::first_end(ends, bytes, length, from),
            // No searcher skips without one.
            None => (false, from, from)
        }
    }
}
